**Verdict — heuristic.** Even granting the RH and GRH claims, I find no \(L_N[\alpha]\), \(\alpha<1/3\), factoring algorithm in the lift construction. There **is** a genuine hidden-prime effect: differentiation on a CM elliptic curve can produce a multiple of an unknown \(p\mid N\), and a gcd can recover \(p\) outright. The obstacle is the derivative order at which this happens. In the explicit example below, one can prove that the coefficient gcd cannot detect \(p\) while \(a+1<p\). Balanced semiprimes already force \(a\) to the scale of \(\sqrt N\).

### A1. What has multiplicity?

**Source statements; RH/GRH unverified.** In classical Stepanov, one constructs a function on a curve whose first \(m\) expansion coefficients vanish at every rational point being counted. Each point then contributes at least \(m\) to the zero divisor. In the remark, the counting target is a *weighted sum over prime powers*. Lean’s `Sz` is \(\sum_{n\le X}\Lambda(n)(X/n-1)\), and `Gz` subtracts its main term. The note describes determinants of automorphic periods that distinguish interlacing weights, first on \(O(2,n)\), then through a chain of unitary groups. I did not locate a statement that a single function has a zero of order \(m\) at each prime power, or a literal double-zero statement for the \(O(2,n)\) prototype. [Remark, pp. 3–4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/remark.txt) · [Formalism/StatementOC.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/StatementOC.lean:11)

Three source statements make the analogy precise enough to test.

- `IsIterLift.step` sends \(u(g)\) to the function of \(h\) obtained by integrating \(u(g)\) against a theta kernel. `stepPar` and `chainPar_succ` describe the corresponding spectral operation: twist the old parameter and append one segment of length \(t\). After \(m\) steps there are \(m\) appended segments. [IteratedLiftChains.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Development/AbelianVarietyTheory/BlockTheory/TwistedDiscreteSeries/IteratedLiftChains.lean:91) · [ChainParSucc.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Development/AbelianVarietyTheory/Definitions/LatticeFrames/ChainParSucc.lean:121)
- `ChainScalar` multiplies the scalars from successive lift–adjoint pairs. `DetChain` says that, under its block hypotheses, \(\det A=\kappa^{\dim E}\prod_{j=1}^m\det B_j\). This is an analogue of the auxiliary polynomial: it assembles many local contributions into one quantity whose size can be bounded. The identity also holds when every factor is a unit. [Formalism/ThetaLiftIndependence/Statement.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/ThetaLiftIndependence/Statement.lean:244)
- There is a literal local vanishing statement. `lemma44_identity_of_raw_tail`, under its hypotheses, gives a polynomial \(F\) whose transformed coefficients match prescribed data modulo \(w^c\) on the identity plane and are all zero modulo \(w^c\) on the other specified planes. Here \(w\) is a uniformizer in a supplied discrete valuation ring over a specified prime. The “point” in this construction is a plane in the local model. The exponent is \(c\); the statement does not identify it with chain length \(m\). [Lemma44IdentityOfRawTail.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/SharpRestriction/GrowthBounds/CoarseMultiplicity/Lemma44IdentityOfRawTail.lean:225) · [CoarseMultiplicityAtIdentity.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/SharpRestriction/GrowthBounds/CoarseMultiplicity/CoarseMultiplicityAtIdentity.lean:78)

### A2. Where does the prime enter?

**KNOWN.** For an elliptic curve in characteristic \(p\), its invariant derivation satisfies \(D^p=H_pD\), where \(H_p\) is the Hasse invariant. At a good inert prime of a CM elliptic curve the reduction is supersingular, so \(H_p=0\). For an integral affine model preserved by \(D\), this gives \(D^p A\subset pA\), hence \(D^{mp}A\subset p^mA\). One can perform all the differentiation modulo \(N^m\) without knowing \(p\). The distinction is between **obtaining divisibility blindly**, which works, and **obtaining it cheaply**, for which the remark supplies no shortcut. [Katz–Mazur, §12.4.1](https://math.bu.edu/people/jsweinst/AWS/Files/KatzMazurArithmeticModuli.pdf)

**Remark claim, partly traced.** The stronger assertion concerns the *value* of the period-normalized derivative \(\Omega^aD^a\theta(0)\) at the CM identity. Its claimed valuation has main term \(a/p+a/p^3+\cdots=ap/(p^2-1)\). Lean’s `jetAt0` explicitly applies a polynomial differential operator at zero, including the factorials, and `oddPowerInvSumIdentity` gives that geometric sum. I did not verify the full theta-value inequality from those statements. Even granting it, the guaranteed valuation grows on the scale \(a/p\). The lift chain is spectral, the derivative divisibility is \(p\)-adic, and the archimedean norms enter the global size comparison. [CartierLocalBounds.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/SharpRestriction/Divisibility/CartierLocalBounds.lean:48) · [QuadraticExponentIdentity.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/GrowthSums/FactorialBaseGrowth/QuadraticExponentIdentity.lean:10)

### A3. The determinant and height ledger

**Proved for the standard lattice.** Write \(p=P+x_0=N^\beta\), \(|x_0|\le B\), and \(f(x)=P+x\). Take the \(d\) rows \(N^{m-i}f^i\) for \(0\le i<m\), followed by \(x^{i-m}f^m\) for \(m\le i<d\). Every row evaluates to a multiple of \(p^m\). Scaling \(x\) by \(B\) gives
\[
\det L=N^{m(m+1)/2}B^{d(d-1)/2}.
\]
A nonzero \(g\) with \(\sqrt d\,\|g(Bx)\|_2<p^m\) must vanish over the integers at \(x_0\). Put \(B=N^\xi\) and \(u=m/d\). To leading order, the determinant certificate requires \(\xi<2\beta u-u^2\le\beta^2\). Thus for balanced factors the certified radius is \(N^{1/4+o(1)}\approx p^{1/2}\). At radius \(N^{1/4+\delta}\), even the optimized determinant scale overshoots the height budget by \(N^{\delta d/2+O(1)}\), before lattice-reduction losses. [Coppersmith, §5](https://cr.yp.to/bib/2001/coppersmith.pdf)

The automorphic ledger bounds a **sum over places** of local indices together with archimedean covolumes. It gives no comparable saving on a nonzero integer polynomial evaluated at \(p-P\). Prop T transfers the repo’s window constructions back to the univariate lattice with polynomial loss in radius. Its scope is those window constructions. And even recovering \(p\) from a smaller *fixed positive fraction* of its top bits would still leave exponentially many prefixes to guess on unhinted input. [Automorphic slope bound `BoundsMu`](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/DivisorInequality/StatementObj.lean:17) · [Prop T](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md:417) · [suffmap §X1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:645)

**Speculative reading of \(2/3\).** `ExplicitFormulaData` displays errors of size \(X/m\) up to \(X^\epsilon\), and a raw division term of size \(m^2b\) entering with denominator \(mb\) and a further scale ratio. An uncancelled \(m^2\) cost balanced against \(X/m\) would give \(m=X^{1/3}\) and error \(X^{2/3}\). This is consistent with the remark’s explanation of why division matters. I could not identify that exact historical calculation. The exponent concerns error in a prime sum. [Formalism/ThickAverageLoss/Statement.lean](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/ThickAverageLoss/Statement.lean:81)

### A4. Computational cost and a finite shadow

**Proved for explicit evaluation.** If \(X=N^\gamma\) for fixed \(\gamma>0\), visiting \(\sqrt X\) stages already costs \(N^{\gamma/2}\). Taking \(X=L_N[\alpha,c]\) makes the stage count affordable at exponent \(\alpha\), but supplies no connection between those stages and either large factor. Lean does give a finite version: `thetaLiftAMF` is a stabilizer-weighted sum over classes, and `ChainInt` clears denominators by multiplying the stage denominators. Neither statement bounds the cost of producing the classes and kernels from \(N\). [Finite lift and integrality statements](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Volumes-git/c1686473-142d-57cc-8202-223c6b26715f/scratchpad/leansrc/lean/src/Formalism/ThetaLiftIndependence/Statement.lean:40)

**Proved; pre-registered toy test.** On \(E:y^2=x^3-x\), use \(Dx=2y,\ Dy=3x^2-1\). For each of the 78 products of two distinct primes in \([67,127]\), the computation received only \(N\) and used the fixed orders \(31,127,255\). Let \(I\) be the product of factors \(3\bmod4\), the inert ones. All 78 matched the predictions: the coefficient gcd with \(N\) was \(1\) at order 31 and \(I\) at order 127. With \(N^2\) it was \(I^2\) at order 255. All 42 mixed pairs split. For \(67\cdot73\), the outputs were \(1,67,4489\). [Runnable scratch test](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/multcopp_c/cm_derivative_test.py)

### A5. What a shortcut would have to do

**Proved reduction.** Write \(D^a x=y^{a\bmod2}P_a(x)\). The two differentiation rules above give \(P_a\) leading coefficient \((a+1)!\) by induction. This proves the early-order obstruction: while \(a+1<p\), at least that coefficient is a unit modulo \(p\). It also gives a precise **single missing lemma** for general factoring: compute the coefficient of the characteristic-zero leading monomial of \(P_a\), reduced modulo \(N\), in \(L_N[\alpha<1/3]\) time for binary \(a\le\sqrt N\). Binary-search the first \(r\) with \(\gcd(r!,N)>1\). That \(r\) is the least prime factor, and \(O(\log N)\) queries suffice. This proposed shortcut already contains fast modular factorial evaluation, the classical Pollard–Strassen route. [Bostan–Gaudry–Schost](https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~eschost/publications/pollard.pdf)

**Heuristic judgment.** My subjective probability that this specific lift/CM route yields classical general factoring below \(L[1/3]\) is **under 1%**. The toy gcd gets an entire factor, so the repo’s one-bit-leak argument would misdiagnose it. Its barrier is the cost of reaching the informative order. The window variant lands in Prop T; the direct derivative variant lands in the repo’s characteristic-dependent degree barrier. Theorem SS would apply only after supplying a family of smooth relations. [CM formal-group barrier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/conn_top.md:143) · [Theorem SS and its hypotheses](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:157)